 ## Background
-Voxel-based representations in neural rendering emerged as a response to the high memory and compute costs of coordinate-based MLPs in NeRF [S1]. Early works like C-NeRF introduced 3D CNNs to generate feature volumes, establishing a baseline for dynamic scene representation . Subsequent methods like FastNeRF and VoxGRAF focused on inference acceleration through caching and sparse grids, respectively [S1]. The family has expanded to handle dynamic scenes (V4D), motion blur (ExBluRF), human avatars (Part-based MHE-augmented NeRF), and generative tasks (VoxGRAF, GSN) [S1]. It is widely used in novel view synthesis, 3D-aware generation, and medical imaging (VoxGS) [S1].
